Date: Mon, 2 Oct 95 11:37:28 MET
From: Gordon McNab <gordon@ayjen1.enet.dec.com>
Subject: RE: ntfs

Andy,

Here is a couple of ways how to make a system with the main installation as 
NTFS:

1) Run ARCINST and make a small partition (~5Mb). This will be your System 
Partition (FAT Format) where the bootloader and HAL will live.

2) Start installing Windows NT and it will ask for the location where you 
actually want to install the main part of Windows NT. It allows you to 
create/delete partitions and/or format them as FAT or NTFS.

OR

1) Run ARCINST and create your large partition (991Mb) THEN create your small 
partition for the bootloader and HAL on the same disk. The last partition 
created by ARCINST will become the System Partition and you will be able to 
re-format the large partition as NTFS during the Windows NT installation.

The second method is the one recommended by Digital in the release notes. The 
reason Windows NT stopped you converting a partition to NTFS was that it 
disallows booting from an NTFS partition, it therefore checks your system 
partition in the ARC Console to see whether it is expected to boot from there. 
Hope this helps.

Gordon

