Subject: Just a few questions

>From: Gill Lapointe <gill.lapointe@the-spa.com>
>Subject: Just a few questions
>
>It is my understanding that the software that is run on an Alpha machine
>running NT does not have to be compiled for Alpha specifically, ie. If it is
>Intel based it will still run.  If this is true what is the difference
>between an Alpha compile and an Intel compile? 
>
As I understand it, any software that does not directly access hardware
will run (may be slow tho'), and if software can be tricked by the HAL 
into thinking that it is accessing the harware when it is not then 
similarly it will run...

It appears however, that specially compiled software (NT specific apps) CAN
bypass the HAL to speak directly to the hardware without upsetting the OS.
When this happens, then we need software that can talk to the hardware
and hence we have different versions of the same software.....

This allows us to use (Non-NT) intel apps on non-intel machines via the HAL
(NT apps try to access intel hardware...crash).

<CUT>
>paraphrase another message (apologies to the original author,  I don't
>recall the actual person)  "Intel based software running on an Alpha would
>yield results equivalent to that of a 486/100."  Is this accurate?
>
>And finally if software is written to an OS,  unless the software makes a
>lot of direct calls to the hardware, why does it matter what processor it is
>run on?  The OS should be the only piece of software that matters. 
>
Hmmm....as I understand it, this was one of the major design benefits and
hence selling points of NT in the first place...and then we get a whole
heap of different platform versions of the NT apps....

dear old microsquigee.....

