Hello Folks,

I think that some of the info provided by Steve could use a little
clarification, and in a couple of cases corrections.

> 
> Hello Gill
> 
> > We have been evaluating the possibility of purchasing some Alpha machines to
> > run NT, and through my research I have developed some questions that maybe
> > someone could answer and help clear up some confusion.
> > 
> > It is my understanding that the software that is run on an Alpha machine
> > running NT does not have to be compiled for Alpha specifically, ie. If it is
> > Intel based it will still run.  If this is true what is the difference
> > between an Alpha compile and an Intel compile? 
> 
> Thats not correct.  The Alpha will run Intel code that runs on a 286 
> machine.  Now when you think about it, who still supports anything on a 286?
> However, the good news is that more amd more applications are being 
> ported to DEC Alpha every day.

This is not really an accurate depiction of what Intel code the Alpha
can emulate.

I don't claim to be an expert on this matter, but here is some info:

The Alpha emulates the 80286 instruction set.  This means that it will
run any application that uses that set.  When the 80386 came out, some
additional instructions were added (for things like protected modes
and memory management).  The 386 instruction set is simply
a super set of the 80286 (actually 8086) instruction set.  So
applications that use 80386 specific instructions will not run on
Alpha.

Many of the applications out there today, do not use 386 specific
code.  You should check with an individual vendor to find out the
capatibility issues.

> 
> Furthermore, it dosen't hurt that Microsoft states that DEC Alpha is the 
> best platform to run NT.
> 
> > In a small attempt to answer this question myself  I contacted one software
> > vendor and posed the question about their product.  They said the only
> > difference between the two versions that they advertise  is the key they
> > send you to connect to the parallel port to unlock the software.  They said
> > if you have the disks used on the Intel machine to use the same disks when
> > installing on the Alpha.
> 

It is probably code that only uses the 80286 instruction set and thus
the Alpha can emulate it.

> Can you tell us the software package?
> 
> > This just made it more confusing.  From my readings in this group I thought
> > I understood that the software should be compiled for Alpha because running
> > software compiled for Intel would not achieve the same performance.  To
> > paraphrase another message (apologies to the original author,  I don't
> > recall the actual person)  "Intel based software running on an Alpha would
> > yield results equivalent to that of a 486/100."  Is this accurate?
> 
> Close.
>  
> > And finally if software is written to an OS,  unless the software makes a
> > lot of direct calls to the hardware, why does it matter what processor it is
> > run on?  The OS should be the only piece of software that matters. 
> 
> Main difference between Intel and Alpha is DEC Alpha is a true 64-bit
> machine.  Intel on the other hand is 32-bit external and 64-bit internal. 

I believe you have this backwards.

When refering to "xx-bit internal" people are generally refering to
the size of the registers (i.e. the size of the chunk of data on which
instructions operate). 
When refering to "xx-bit external" people are generally refering to
the width of the data bus.

So using that terminology, Alpha is a 64-bit internal/128-bit external
machine, while Intel Pentium is a 32-bit internal/64-bit external machine.

Here are some more specific specs:

Alpha ev4/ev45 (21064):
	43-bit virtual addressing (34-bits physical)
	128-bit data bus
	64-bit general purpose registers
	dual issue (can execute 2 instructions per clock cycle)
        ev4 has 8KB data & 8KB instruction L1 on-chip cache
	ev45 has 16KB data & 16KB instruction L1 on-chip cache
	frequency range up to 275MHz (ev45)
Alpha ev5 (21164):
	43-bit virtual addressing (40-bit physical)
	128-bit data bus
	64-bit general purpose registers
	quad issue (can execute 4 instructions per clock cycle)
	8KB data & 8KB instruction L1 on-chip cache
	96KB combined data & instruction L2 *on-chip* cache
	frequency range up to 300MHz
Intel (pentium):
	46-bit virtual addressing (32-bit physical)
	64-bit data bus
	32-bit general purpose registers
	dual issue (can execute 2 instructions per clock cycle)
	8KB data & 8KB instruction L1 on-chip cache
	frequency range up to 133MHz?

> Now consider how a DEC Alpha handles an instruction cycle, on the 21064 
> chip it does a dual fetch on a 64-bit word.
> 
> However, the new 21164 chip processes four 256-bit words per clock 
> cycle.

This is incorrect.  The 21164 can execute 4 quadwords (64-bit word)
per clock cycle.

> But as far as I know no one has yet found a way to take 
> adavantage of such a large chunk of data.  Course, I may be wrong, its 
> happen before.:->
> 

Actually, my understanding is that Oracle has been re-written to
specifically take advantage of the 64-bit capabilities of the Alpha.
You would have to check with them for details.

> Anyway if you need a DEC apps list for NT on Alpha I have one that I'd be 
> happy to send you.
> 

cheers,
Kevin

