Subject: Printing to NT from Unix

Hi,

I have a problem with printing postscript files from unix to a WindowsNT queue.

We currently have an AlphaServer 1000 4/233 running WindowsNT server 3.5,
services for Macintosh and TCP/IP printing services.  I created a printer
queue on the NT server which points to a Postscript printer.  This queue
works fine for our Mac, Windows 3. 11 and NT workstation 3.5 users. 

However, we would also like our unix users to print to this printer via
NT.  Our unix users are able to print text files OK, as the NT print
processor converts plain text to postscript.  The problem is that the NT
print processor does the same to postscript files.  In other words, the
print processor sees the unix queued postscript code as plain text and
promptly converts it to postscript.  The result is a lot of pages with
postscript code on them. 

Our unix machines are a mixture of DECstations running Ultirx v4.4 and
Alphas running OSF/1 v2. 

My question is: How do I make the print processor destinguish between
plain text and postscript files, and only convert the plain text files. 

Thanks

Stuart Dainton
Computer Systems Officer
James Cook University - Cairns Campus
Email: stuart.dainton@jcu.edu.au

From jcrozier@mail.dos.state.fl.us Thu Oct 12 06:39:00 1995 CDT
Message-ID: <9509128135.AA813508781@mail.dos.state.fl.us>
Date: Thu, 12 Oct 95 07:37:03 est
From: Crozier, Jim <jcrozier@mail.dos.state.fl.us>
Subject: Re: Printing to NT from Unix

I have seen problems of this type before and I suspect that is may 
     have nothing to do with NT, but with the way that the Unix station is 
     presenting the PS file.
     
     Some PostScript drivers will insert a ^D or other character before the 
     PS "magic cookie" thus causing the NT print service to not recognize 
     the file as a PS file.
     
     If you have not examined the output of the PS driver on the Unix 
     station, I would look at that first.
     
     If this turns out to be that problem,  there is probably an option 
     somewhere to turn it off - I have no idea what it might be called.
     
     Good luck, let me know.
     
     Jim Crozier - Fl Dept of State

