From aaron@garply.com Thu Oct 26 01:41:50 1995 CDT
Message-ID: <9510260641.AA30035@foo.garply.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Oct 1995 01:41:50 -0500
From: Aaron Sakovich <aaron@garply.com>
Subject: Intel compatibility update!

Hi all,

I ran across a letter to the editor in the latest issue of Digital News and
Review from the (in)famous Benn Schreiber (Hi Benn, if you're reading this!),
the manager of the NT systems group at DECwet, uh, west.

In it, he makes four points, three of which are welcome confirmation to us
Alpha users.  I'll quote all four here:

> 1) Win16 applications that do not use enhanced mode run unchanged today.
> 2) Win16 applications that use enhanced mode will run when the "Shell
>    Update Release" ships next spring.
> 3) We are exploring the ability to run Intel Win32 applications unchanged, 
>    and should have something to say about this soon.
> 4) All of the above are emulated, meaning that they run slower because the
>    instructions are interpreted in some way.  We are exploring binary
>    program-translation techniques (similar to that used on OpenVMS to get
>    from VAX to Alpha, and the "Freeport" program that translates Sun
>    applications to run on Digital Unix. (sic)  These techniques will
>    provide the ability to run Intel Win32 applications at very high levels
>    of performance.  Technology previews of this capability were demonstrated
>    at Spring Comdex a year or two ago.

I'm just sorry that it's taken so long to get the translator out -- OpenVMS
and Digital UNIX (nee, OSF/1) had it years ago when Alpha first arrived.  It's
a shame NT users have had to wait sooooo long.

Ah, a faithful and loyal lot we are...

(But think how many more of us there'd have been if the translator were
available two years ago!!!)

Aaron

